<exercises xml:id="ex-7-2-Right_triangle_trig" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
	<title component="not-workbook">Exercises</title>

<introduction component="not-workbook">
    <p>
        Exercises 1-4 are available in a separate workbook format.
    </p>
</introduction>

<!-- rtt stands for right triangle trig -->
<exercise xml:id="giraffe-rtt"> 
    <statement>
        <p>
            Giraffes stretch their long necks to reach high leaves on trees. Kirin, a giraffe at our local zoo, boasts a 6 foot long neck. He can extend his neck at an angle of 55<degree/> (relative to his shoulder level) to feed on high acacia branches. If Kirin's shoulder stands 11 feet above the ground, how high a leaf can he reach?
        </p>
    </statement>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="radio-tower-rtt">
    <introduction>
        <p>
            A 300 foot cable is tethered from the top of a radio tower to the ground.
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                The cable forms a 40<degree/> angle with the tower. What is the height of the tower and how far away from the tower is the cable tethered to the ground?
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                The company wants to tether another 300 foot cable to a nearby tower that is only 185 feet tall.  What angle will the cable make with the tower?
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="tv-mounting-rtt">
    <introduction>
        <p>
            Zoha wants to mount her television on the wall of her apartment. She sits down on her couch and measures two distances.  The horizontal distance from her eyes to the wall is 104<dblprime/> and the vertical distance from her eyes to the floor is 43<dblprime/>.
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                To prevent neck strain, Zoha wants the angle from her eyes (when she's sitting on the couch) up to the top edge of the television screen to be no more than 15<degree/>. When she mounts the television on the wall, how many inches up the wall should the top of the screen be?
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Zoha's television is <q>75<dblprime/> diagonal</q>, which means it is 37<dblprime/> tall. What is the angle from her eyes (when she's sitting on the couch) down to the bottom edge of the television screen?  
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                To prevent neck strain, the angle you found in part (b) should also be 15<degree/> or less. Is it, or should Zoha consider buying a smaller television?
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="special-triangles-rtt">
    <introduction>
        <p>
            In Section 7.1 we saw two special triangles that came from Tim's observations about the angles of branches of maple and linden trees in his neighborhood. We can use those triangles to evaluate the trigonometric ratios at 30<degree/>, 45<degree/>, and 60<degree/>. Here are the two special triangles we found
        </p>

        <sidebyside widths="45% 45%">
            <image>
                <latex-image xml:id="tikz-7-2-30-60-90">
                \begin{tikzpicture}
                    % Coordinates:
                    % Side \sqrt{3} (horizontal bottom): length = 1.732 * 3 ≈ 5.196
                    % Side 1 (vertical to the right): length = 3
                    \coordinate (A) at (1.732*3, 0);    % Right-angle corner (bottom-right)
                    \coordinate (B) at (0, 0);          % Bottom-left corner
                    \coordinate (C) at (1.732*3, 3);    % Top-right corner
    
                    % Draw Triangle
                    \draw[thick] (A) -- (B) -- (C) node[midway, sloped, above] {2} -- cycle;
    
                    % Right angle symbol (at bottom-right)
                    \draw (1.732*3 - 0.2, 0) -- (1.732*3 - 0.2, 0.2) -- (1.732*3, 0.2);
    
                    % Bottom-left 30° angle arc and label
                    \draw (0.5, 0) arc (0:30:0.5);
                    \node at (1.3, 0.3) {$30^\circ$};
    
                    % Top-right 60° angle arc and label
                    \draw (1.732*3, 3 - 0.5) arc (270:210:0.5);
                    \node at (1.732*3 - 0.5, 2.2) {$60^\circ$};
    
                    % Side Labels
                    \node[below] at (1.732*1.5, 0) {$\sqrt{3}$};
                    \node[right] at (1.732*3, 1.5) {1};
                \end{tikzpicture}
                </latex-image>
            </image>
    
            <image>
                <latex-image xml:id="tikz-7-2-45-45-90">
                \begin{tikzpicture}
                    % Coordinates for 45-45-90 isosceles right triangle (legs of equal length 3)
                    \coordinate (A) at (3, 0);    % Right-angle corner (bottom-right)
                    \coordinate (B) at (0, 0);    % Bottom-left corner (45° angle)
                    \coordinate (C) at (3, 3);    % Top-right corner (45° angle)
    
                    % Draw Triangle with Hypotenuse Label
                    \draw[thick] (A) -- (B) -- (C) node[midway, sloped, above] {$\sqrt{2}$} -- cycle;
    
                    % Right angle symbol (at bottom-right)
                    \draw (3 - 0.2, 0) -- (3 - 0.2, 0.2) -- (3, 0.2);
    
                    % Bottom-left 45° angle arc and label
                    \draw (0.5, 0) arc (0:45:0.5);
                    \node at (1.1, 0.3) {$45^\circ$};
    
                    % Top-right 45° angle arc and label
                    \draw (3, 3 - 0.5) arc (270:225:0.5);
                    \node at (3 - 0.5, 2.1) {$45^\circ$};
    
                    % Side Labels
                    \node[below] at (1.5, 0) {1};
                    \node[right] at (3, 1.5) {1};
                \end{tikzpicture}
                </latex-image>
            </image>
    
        </sidebyside>
    </introduction>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Use the first triangle to evaluate <m>\sin(30^{\circ})</m>, <m>\cos(30^{\circ})</m>, and <m>\tan(30^{\circ})</m>. Write your answers as exact fractions (which might involve square roots) and give decimal approximations. Hint: The side opposite to 30<degree/> has length 1 while the side adjacent to 30<degree/> has length <m>\sqrt{3}</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Use the first triangle again to evaluate <m>\sin(60^{\circ})</m>, <m>\cos(60^{\circ})</m>, and <m>\tan(60^{\circ})</m>. Write your answers as exact fractions (which might involve square roots) and give decimal approximations. Caution: The side opposite to 60<degree/> has length <m>\sqrt{3}</m> while the side adjacent to 60<degree/> has length 1. (Lesson learned: which leg is opposite and which leg is adjacent depends on which angle we are using.)
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Use the second triangle to evaluate <m>\sin(45^{\circ})</m>, <m>\cos(45^{\circ})</m>, and <m>\tan(45^{\circ})</m>. Write your answers as exact fractions (which might involve square roots) and give decimal approximations. Hint: the side opposite to 45<degree/> and the side adjacent to 45<degree/> each are length 1.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="robots-rtt" component="not-workbook">
    <introduction>
        <p>
            At a local warehouse, most of the work grabbing goods and packing them into shipping crates is done by high-speed <q>pick-and-place</q> robots.  Each robot works above a ramped conveyor belt that moves packages into shipping crates.  The conveyor belt has a rail that extends the robots arm horizontally to push packages up the ramp.  The standard rail goes out up to 4 feet.
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                One robot is assigned a ramp that makes an angle 18<degree/> with the floor. What is the height of the platform at the top of the ramp? Assume the robot uses the full 4 foot horizontal extension from the rail.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Another robot is assigned a ramp that reaches a larger box, so the height of the platform at the top of the ramp is 30 inches.  What angle does the ramp make with the floor?  Again, assume the robot uses the full 4 foot horizontal extension from the rail.  Hint: start by converting 30 inches to decimal feet.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="airplane-rtt" component="not-workbook">
    <statement>
        <p>
            An airplane is getting ready to land at the airport. Air Traffic control has authorized a descent path of 50 miles along a 3<degree/> glide slope, meaning the angle between the path the airplane travels and the ground is 3<degree/>. At what horizontal distance from the airport would the airplane begin its descent?
        </p>
    </statement>
    
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="sciatica-rtt" component="not-workbook">
    <introduction>
        <p>
            Rafael is a physical therapist.  His patient, a 65-year old woman, is complaining of sciatic nerve pain.
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                During the intake appointment, Rafael asked his patient to lay down on the examination table and keep both her legs straight but relaxed.  Rafael lifted her right leg and stopped when the nerve pain started.  At that point, the patient's heel was 23 inches above the table.  The patient's leg is 29 inches long.  What is the hip flexion (angle) of the patient's leg relative to the table at the point where the pain starts? (By the way, this measurement is called the Straight Leg Raise (SLR) test.)
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                After 6 weeks of physical therapy, Rafael repeated the assessment. The goal is to be able to get his patient's leg to to 70<degree/> hip flexion (angle) without pain. How high off the table would Rafael need to lift his patient's heel without pain to to reach this goal?
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="sonar-rtt">
    <introduction>
        <p>
            An oceanographic survey ship uses sonar (sound) to map a deep-sea trench. The sonar beam is aimed at an underwater ridge at an angle of <m>35^{\circ}</m> measured from the vertical line dropping directly down from the ship. The ridge is located at a horizontal distance of 840 meters from the ship.
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                What is the depth of the ocean floor at the ridge?
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                If another trench section is known to be 1,500 meters deep, at what angle from the vertical should the team direct their sonar beam to hit a feature 900 meters horizontally away?
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
</exercise>

</exercises>